01:13:19.010 --> 01:13:20.550
Was butterfly your main-
And this was at the age,

1699
01:13:20.550 --> 01:13:22.363
yeah, I was a butterfly swimmer.

1700
01:13:23.240 --> 01:13:26.230
And this was a period of time in the late 1980s

1701
01:13:26.230 --> 01:13:31.030
where swimming was just starting to figure out,

1702
01:13:31.030 --> 01:13:33.400
competitive swimmers were starting to figure out

1703
01:13:33.400 --> 01:13:36.780
how powerful the underwater dolphin kick was off the walls

1704
01:13:36.780 --> 01:13:38.960
and how you could swim faster underwater

1705
01:13:38.960 --> 01:13:43.210
with a proper dolphin than you could on the surface.

1706
01:13:43.210 --> 01:13:46.540
So there were backstrokers,

1707
01:13:46.540 --> 01:13:48.853
starting with Dave Berkoff at Harvard,

1708
01:13:49.850 --> 01:13:51.330
who would essentially swim

1709
01:13:51.330 --> 01:13:54.610
almost the entire 100-meter backstroke underwater,

1710
01:13:54.610 --> 01:13:55.840
'cause they could go so much faster.

1711
01:13:55.840 --> 01:13:57.710
And American and world records

1712
01:13:57.710 --> 01:14:00.880
were being broken using this technique.

1713
01:14:00.880 --> 01:14:05.880
Until FINA and USA Swimming said, hold on a second,

1714
01:14:06.450 --> 01:14:08.630
this is rewriting the entire thing.

1715
01:14:08.630 --> 01:14:11.130
We're not so sure this is the best way to do this.

1716
01:14:11.130 --> 01:14:13.338
So they ended up instituting a rule that said

1717
01:14:13.338 --> 01:14:17.010
you could not swim underwater dolphin kick

1718
01:14:17.010 --> 01:14:18.957
for more than 15 meters off each wall.

1719
01:14:18.957 --> 01:14:20.960
And that kind of changed the game.

1720
01:14:20.960 --> 01:14:24.010
But during this period of time where everybody

1721
01:14:24.010 --> 01:14:26.350
was pushing the limits with the dolphin kick,

1722
01:14:26.350 --> 01:14:29.350
we were experimenting with it on the team.

1723
01:14:29.350 --> 01:14:32.090
And I was always very strong in this regard.

1724
01:14:32.090 --> 01:14:35.840
So the monofin was like, where has this been my whole life?

1725
01:14:35.840 --> 01:14:36.990
I love this.

1726
01:14:36.990 --> 01:14:39.970
Maybe I should get into this finswimming thing.

1727
01:14:39.970 --> 01:14:43.170
And Pablo, myself, and a couple others along,

1728
01:14:43.170 --> 01:14:47.727
with John Mix's leadership started to learn more

1729
01:14:48.690 --> 01:14:51.360
about this world of competitive finswimming,

1730
01:14:51.360 --> 01:14:53.120
which for people that don't know,

1731
01:14:53.120 --> 01:14:55.370
there is an entire sport and subculture

1732
01:14:55.370 --> 01:14:59.690
that's quite robust in certain parts of Europe

1733
01:14:59.690 --> 01:15:04.690
and particularly in Russia and in parts of China where,

1734
01:15:04.770 --> 01:15:06.660
when you're coming up as a kid

1735
01:15:06.660 --> 01:15:08.510
and you show prowess in the pool,

1736
01:15:08.510 --> 01:15:11.790
they figure out right away, very early,

1737
01:15:11.790 --> 01:15:13.710
whether you're gonna be a fin swimmer

1738
01:15:13.710 --> 01:15:16.270
or a traditional competitive swimmer.

1739
01:15:16.270 --> 01:15:19.680
And certain kids get channeled into this finswimming world.

1740
01:15:19.680 --> 01:15:22.140
And there's a whole universe

1741
01:15:22.140 --> 01:15:25.340
of international finswimming competition,

1742
01:15:25.340 --> 01:15:28.527
where they have these meets, and you're in this monofin,

1743
01:15:28.527 --> 01:15:33.527
and you basically dive in off of a starting block

1744
01:15:33.830 --> 01:15:36.050
just like you would in traditional swimming.

1745
01:15:36.050 --> 01:15:39.343
And you swim underwater as fast as you can with this fin.

1746
01:15:40.898 --> 01:15:43.010
Everything from 50 meters-
And snorkle, right?

1747
01:15:43.010 --> 01:15:44.770
Yeah, so with some of the events.

1748
01:15:44.770 --> 01:15:46.050
Every event is different.

1749
01:15:46.050 --> 01:15:49.280
It's divided up into all these fascinating subcategories.

1750
01:15:49.280 --> 01:15:53.780
So if you're just swimming the 50 meters,

1751
01:15:53.780 --> 01:15:55.830
you don't need anything, because literally,

1752
01:15:55.830 --> 01:15:57.660
they cover the distance in like I don't know,

1753
01:15:57.660 --> 01:15:59.120
I should have looked up what the current

1754
01:15:59.120 --> 01:16:03.010
world record is for 50 meters, but it's like, I don't know,

1755
01:16:03.010 --> 01:16:05.580
12 seconds or 13 seconds or something like that.

1756
01:16:05.580 --> 01:16:08.950
And when these swimmers dive in with the fin,

1757
01:16:08.950 --> 01:16:12.500
this wave, it's like a tsunami, like a low,

1758
01:16:12.500 --> 01:16:16.920
like a two foot tsunami just moves down the 50-meter pool

1759
01:16:16.920 --> 01:16:19.790
and then just drenches all the officials

1760
01:16:19.790 --> 01:16:21.000
at the other end of the pool.

1761
01:16:21.000 --> 01:16:22.580
And it happens so quickly.

1762
01:16:22.580 --> 01:16:24.330
It's fascinating to watch.

1763
01:16:24.330 --> 01:16:27.713
There are other events where you use a snorkel.

1764
01:16:28.780 --> 01:16:31.570
So you have a center-mounted snorkel.

1765
01:16:31.570 --> 01:16:33.670
You dive in, and then you swim just below

1766
01:16:33.670 --> 01:16:35.930
the surface of the water, but the snorkel is above,

1767
01:16:35.930 --> 01:16:37.770
so you can breathe the entire time.

1768
01:16:37.770 --> 01:16:39.970
And you're in a streamlined position like this

1769
01:16:39.970 --> 01:16:42.980
with your hands out in front of you, undulating.

1770
01:16:42.980 --> 01:16:47.870
And then there are other events, longer events where,

1771
01:16:47.870 --> 01:16:49.460
I forget which one is apnea.

1772
01:16:49.460 --> 01:16:50.700
Is that where you have the snorkel

1773
01:16:50.700 --> 01:16:51.850
or is that where you have the tank?

1774
01:16:51.850 --> 01:16:54.020
No, apnea is breath hold.

1775
01:16:54.020 --> 01:16:56.530
So that's the 50, like when you just do the breath hold.

1776
01:16:56.530 --> 01:16:58.260
There are other ones where you use a tank.

1777
01:16:58.260 --> 01:17:00.860
You literally have an oxygen tank

1778
01:17:00.860 --> 01:17:02.730
that is wired to your mouth.
Really?

1779
01:17:02.730 --> 01:17:04.360
A thin long oxygen tank,

1780
01:17:04.360 --> 01:17:06.240
and you hold it out in front of you

1781
01:17:06.240 --> 01:17:08.670
like this in the streamline position.

1782
01:17:08.670 --> 01:17:11.290
I have my hands above my head for people that are listening.

1783
01:17:11.290 --> 01:17:14.350
And you'll swim 1,500 meters underwater

1784
01:17:14.350 --> 01:17:16.320
without ever breaking the surface,

1785
01:17:16.320 --> 01:17:19.470
breathing by dent of this oxygen tank.

1786
01:17:19.470 --> 01:17:21.450
You ready for the world record, 50 meters?

1787
01:17:21.450 --> 01:17:22.290
Oh, did you just look it up?

1788
01:17:22.290 --> 01:17:23.560
What is it?
15 seconds.

1789
