15 seconds, all right.

1790
01:17:24.477 --> 01:17:27.530
And 100 meters is 33.87.

1791
01:17:27.530 --> 01:17:29.570
So it's crazy.

1792
01:17:29.570 --> 01:17:31.070
When I discovered this, I was like,

1793
01:17:31.070 --> 01:17:33.203
how did I never know about this?

1794
01:17:34.850 --> 01:17:37.900
Because it's never really made its way to America.

1795
01:17:37.900 --> 01:17:40.790
So John brings the monofin back.

1796
01:17:40.790 --> 01:17:45.790
He introduces this device to a group of elite swimmers.

1797
01:17:46.290 --> 01:17:49.280
In short shrift, it becomes a go-to training device

1798
01:17:49.280 --> 01:17:53.370
for a lot of very elite competitive swimmers.

1799
01:17:53.370 --> 01:17:58.130
Most prominently, there's a swimmer called Misty Hyman,

1800
01:17:58.130 --> 01:18:00.190
who, she ended up winning

1801
01:18:00.190 --> 01:18:03.453
the 200-meter butterfly gold medal in Sydney.

1802
01:18:04.759 --> 01:18:08.910
And she was a devoted practitioner of using the monofin

1803
01:18:08.910 --> 01:18:11.690
in training and she had an unbelievable

1804
01:18:11.690 --> 01:18:15.008
underwater dolphin kick, the best in the world.

1805
01:18:15.008 --> 01:18:17.850
She's probably the most prominent example.

1806
01:18:17.850 --> 01:18:20.500
But that got John thinking about how

1807
01:18:20.500 --> 01:18:23.010
he could put together a team in the United States

1808
01:18:23.010 --> 01:18:25.660
to go to the world championships of finswimming.

1809
01:18:25.660 --> 01:18:27.670
I don't think I've ever told this story on the podcast,

1810
01:18:27.670 --> 01:18:31.673
but in, I think it was 1996,

1811
01:18:36.000 --> 01:18:37.850
the world championships in finswimming

1812
01:18:37.850 --> 01:18:39.980
was gonna be held in Hungary

1813
01:18:39.980 --> 01:18:43.280
in this town outside of Budapest called Dunaujvaros.

1814
01:18:43.280 --> 01:18:44.427
I'm sure I'm saying that wrong.

1815
01:18:44.427 --> 01:18:46.084
No, that's a great pronunciation.

1816
01:18:46.084 --> 01:18:47.265
Dunaujvaros.
Dunaujvaros.

1817
01:18:47.265 --> 01:18:49.840
I'm sure I'm saying it incorrectly.

1818
01:18:49.840 --> 01:18:51.210
It sounds good.

1819
01:18:51.210 --> 01:18:56.210
And he puts together this team.

1820
01:18:56.800 --> 01:18:59.430
There were probably like 10 or 12 of us.

1821
01:18:59.430 --> 01:19:01.570
The team included Jenny Thompson,

1822
01:19:01.570 --> 01:19:05.130
who between 1992 and 2004 won more Olympic medals

1823
01:19:05.130 --> 01:19:07.360
than any other female swimmer in history.

1824
01:19:07.360 --> 01:19:10.820
Pablo Morales, most winning swimmer in NCAA history,

1825
01:19:10.820 --> 01:19:13.270
won the gold in the 100 fly in Athens.

1826
01:19:13.270 --> 01:19:14.380
Your friend.
Misty Hyman,

1827
01:19:14.380 --> 01:19:16.030
who I just mentioned.

1828
01:19:16.030 --> 01:19:18.670
Chris Morgan, my friend, Chris Morgan,

1829
01:19:18.670 --> 01:19:22.900
who went on to become a very prominent swim coach.

1830
01:19:22.900 --> 01:19:26.610
He was the 2008 Olympic coach for Switzerland in Beijing.

1831
01:19:26.610 --> 01:19:29.250
He then went on to be assistant coach at Harvard.

1832
01:19:29.250 --> 01:19:31.010
He's doing other things in the swimming world now.

1833
01:19:31.010 --> 01:19:32.590
And then me, right?

1834
01:19:32.590 --> 01:19:35.033
I was sort of like piggybacked onto this crowd.

1835
01:19:35.880 --> 01:19:36.713
The would-be

1836
01:19:36.713 --> 01:19:37.546
lawyer waving his-
'Cause I was hanging

1837
01:19:37.546 --> 01:19:39.000
around Pablo.
Good dolphin kick.

1838
01:19:39.000 --> 01:19:41.200
John's like, "Yeah, you can come."

1839
01:19:41.200 --> 01:19:44.010
So in '96, we all go.

1840
01:19:44.010 --> 01:19:47.030
I'm like with these incredible Olympians.

1841
01:19:47.030 --> 01:19:50.420
We go to Hungary to compete in this meet,

1842
01:19:50.420 --> 01:19:52.513
but we don't know what we're doing at all.

1843
01:19:53.530 --> 01:19:54.910
These swimmers are unbelievable,

1844
01:19:54.910 --> 01:19:56.860
but they have no experience in finswimming,

1845
01:19:56.860 --> 01:19:59.780
which we soon learn is a very different discipline.

1846
01:19:59.780 --> 01:20:01.890
We were training together and trying to figure it out,

1847
01:20:01.890 --> 01:20:04.937
but it was like a hilarious,

1848
01:20:04.937 --> 01:20:07.680
"Bad News Bears"-type situation,

1849
01:20:07.680 --> 01:20:09.930
but with Olympians, with gold metal,

1850
01:20:09.930 --> 01:20:11.430
world record-setting Olympians.

1851
01:20:11.430 --> 01:20:13.968
Did they liked the fact that they were in beginner mode?

1852
01:20:13.968 --> 01:20:15.130
Was it-
No, 'cause it was fun.

1853
01:20:15.130 --> 01:20:16.230
There was no pressure.

1854
01:20:17.391 --> 01:20:20.790
And the United States had never attended one of these meets.

1855
01:20:20.790 --> 01:20:25.290
So we show up in Hungary, we go to this town,

1856
01:20:25.290 --> 01:20:28.848
which is kind of a communist relic,

1857
01:20:28.848 --> 01:20:33.443
a lot of these tall sort of housing facilities

1858
01:20:34.500 --> 01:20:37.483
that you could tell are from a bygone era.

1859
01:20:38.840 --> 01:20:39.990
And a very industrial town.

1860
01:20:39.990 --> 01:20:43.570
I think the steel mill was really the engine

1861
01:20:43.570 --> 01:20:47.100
for the economy in this part of the world.

1862
01:20:47.100 --> 01:20:49.190
But they have this beautiful natatorium.

1863
01:20:50.373 --> 01:20:52.520
We swim in this meet,

1864
01:20:52.520 --> 01:20:55.900
and I was like on the 4x200 relay.

1865
01:20:55.900 --> 01:20:58.100
They do relays like they do in swimming.

1866
01:20:58.100 --> 01:21:00.160
And just watching it was fascinating.

1867
01:21:00.160 --> 01:21:03.100
And meeting all of these athletes from all over the world.

1868
01:21:03.100 --> 01:21:05.950
I remember, there was one guy, the guy who,

1869
01:21:05.950 --> 01:21:09.090
I think he won the 50-meter apnea

1870
01:21:09.090 --> 01:21:12.110
was a dude from China who kind of was,

1871
01:21:12.110 --> 01:21:16.000
he was a big dude, kind of bulky, he had like a belly,

1872
01:21:16.000 --> 01:21:19.117
and he was smoking cigarettes outside the auditorium

1873
01:21:20.140 --> 01:21:22.010
in between sessions.
(laughs) Badass.

1874
01:21:22.010 --> 01:21:24.200
But he would dive in and like break

1875
01:21:24.200 --> 01:21:26.090
the world record in the 50 meters,

1876
01:21:26.090 --> 01:21:28.483
swimming in a way that none of us could.

1877
01:21:29.690 --> 01:21:34.458
And it was just really, it was just a wild, wild experience.

1878
01:21:34.458 --> 01:21:35.787
They're like, "Sir, your meet's about to start."

1879
01:21:35.787 --> 01:21:36.787
"Nah, I'll be fine."

1880
01:21:37.890 --> 01:21:38.950
But what's interesting,

1881
01:21:38.950 --> 01:21:41.750
and the reason I bring it up is that the legacy

1882
01:21:41.750 --> 01:21:45.960
of that experience is FINIS kind of popularizing,

1883
01:21:45.960 --> 01:21:49.170
through John, popularizing the monofin as a training device.

1884
01:21:49.170 --> 01:21:51.580
And most competitive swimmers use it

1885
01:21:51.580 --> 01:21:53.880
as one of the tools in their toolkit.

1886
01:21:53.880 --> 01:21:55.300
But at the same time,

1887
01:21:55.300 --> 01:21:58.630
finswimming has never caught on in the United States.

1888
01:21:58.630 --> 01:22:00.180
And you have some experience with this, right?

1889
01:22:00.180 --> 01:22:01.240
Because there's a crossover

1890
01:22:01.240 --> 01:22:03.170
between the finswimming world

1891
01:22:03.170 --> 01:22:04.280
and the freediving world.

1892
01:22:04.280 --> 01:22:09.280
Yes, so the monofin that was adopted by free divers

1893
01:22:10.410 --> 01:22:13.240
to go on constant way to get the records,

1894
01:22:13.240 --> 01:22:14.900
originally, they were just using bifins,

1895
01:22:14.900 --> 01:22:17.800
and then someone found the monofin and incorporated that.

1896
01:22:17.800 --> 01:22:22.214
And Alexey Molchanov was groomed

1897
01:22:22.214 --> 01:22:26.020
as a high-end national team level swimmer

1898
01:22:26.020 --> 01:22:27.240
from the time he's a little kid,

1899
01:22:27.240 --> 01:22:30.530
because his mother Natalia, the greatest freediver ever,

1900
01:22:30.530 --> 01:22:33.660
she was a competitive swimmer

1901
01:22:33.660 --> 01:22:35.800
for the first 24 years of her life.

1902
01:22:35.800 --> 01:22:40.800
Anyway, Alexi was kind of put

1903
01:22:41.096 --> 01:22:43.310
through the sports school in Russia.

1904
01:22:43.310 --> 01:22:45.660
And just like you said, he was divided,

1905
01:22:45.660 --> 01:22:47.860
put into the finswimming camp,

1906
01:22:47.860 --> 01:22:50.300
probably 'cause he was built with amazing powerful legs

1907
01:22:50.300 --> 01:22:51.860
and he had a great dolphin kick,

1908
01:22:51.860 --> 01:22:53.280
and they put him right in the finswimming.

1909
01:22:53.280 --> 01:22:55.050
And he did finswimming until he was 17

1910
01:22:55.050 --> 01:22:57.347
and immediately went to freediving from that.

1911
01:22:57.347 --> 01:22:58.950
And it gave him this advantage.

1912
01:22:58.950 --> 01:23:01.310
Now he's the deepest ever with a monofin.

1913
01:23:01.310 --> 01:23:02.970
Well, there's a reason, yeah.

1914
01:23:02.970 --> 01:23:04.600
Yeah, it's super interesting.

1915
01:23:04.600 --> 01:23:09.380
I can tell that when you're fit and you're using a monofin,

1916
01:23:09.380 --> 01:23:11.560
the feeling of moving through the water

1917
01:23:11.560 --> 01:23:15.030
that fast underwater is exhilarating.

1918
01:23:15.030 --> 01:23:18.010
It's crazy how fast you can go under water with it.

1919
01:23:18.010 --> 01:23:18.890
And there are, like I said,

1920
01:23:18.890 --> 01:23:19.920
there's different monofins

1921
01:23:19.920 --> 01:23:21.410
for different events and purposes.

1922
01:23:21.410 --> 01:23:24.280
So for the longer distances,

1923
01:23:24.280 --> 01:23:25.730
you're gonna have a very large,

1924
01:23:25.730 --> 01:23:29.920
broad, pliable monofin that has a lot of give in it.

1925
01:23:29.920 --> 01:23:32.100
And for like the 50-meter apnea,

1926
01:23:32.100 --> 01:23:34.110
it's gonna be a very stiff board.

1927
01:23:34.110 --> 01:23:37.750
And so I don't know what it's like now, but back then,

1928
01:23:37.750 --> 01:23:42.750
you could get a sort of commercially manufactured fin

1929
01:23:42.810 --> 01:23:44.690
that would just be a piece of fiberglass.

1930
01:23:44.690 --> 01:23:49.140
But there's real artistry in these people,

1931
01:23:49.140 --> 01:23:51.540
much like surfboards, creating the perfect fin

1932
01:23:51.540 --> 01:23:55.340
for that individual and for a specific distance or event,

1933
01:23:55.340 --> 01:23:58.030
that tapers, and there's all these little,

1934
01:23:58.030 --> 01:24:00.810
certain peccadilloes that the different athletes

1935
01:24:00.810 --> 01:24:02.280
would have with respect to their fins.

1936
01:24:02.280 --> 01:24:05.650
And so we were trading fins and trading T-shirts

1937
01:24:05.650 --> 01:24:09.210
with the Russians and the Chinese, and it was wild.

1938
01:24:09.210 --> 01:24:12.460
And as a swimmer who never made it to the level

1939
01:24:12.460 --> 01:24:14.230
where I was competing internationally,

1940
01:24:14.230 --> 01:24:17.630
it was like this cool way of tasting that

1941
01:24:18.470 --> 01:24:20.547
in a very low stakes environment for us.

1942
01:24:20.547 --> 01:24:22.820
'Cause we didn't win any medals or-

1943
01:24:22.820 --> 01:24:24.510
Were you still in law school?

1944
01:24:24.510 --> 01:24:27.570
This was, yeah, I was still, yeah, or maybe not.

1945
01:24:27.570 --> 01:24:32.570
I think it was right after I graduated.

1946
01:24:32.580 --> 01:24:35.560
Maybe it was, '94, I can't remember.

1947
01:24:35.560 --> 01:24:38.300
No, it was after law school, but shortly thereafter.

1948
01:24:38.300 --> 01:24:39.560
Fun.
As you get old,

1949
01:24:39.560 --> 01:24:41.460
I can't remember the timeline exactly.

1950
01:24:42.400 --> 01:24:44.820
Do you miss, how come you haven't,

1951
01:24:44.820 --> 01:24:46.870
you got to try freediving with the monofin, man.

1952
01:24:46.870 --> 01:24:48.980
You got to get your monofin back.

1953
01:24:48.980 --> 01:24:50.840
Well, listen, when I was in Malta,

1954
01:24:50.840 --> 01:24:53.950
I did a whole day of freediving breath training

1955
01:24:53.950 --> 01:24:55.657
with that guy, I can't remember his name.

1956
01:24:55.657 --> 01:24:58.220
The guy lives there, who's like a former world champion.

1957
01:24:58.220 --> 01:25:02.110
And then we went out to actually freedive,

1958
01:25:02.110 --> 01:25:05.360
and I could not get down below 12 or 15 feet

1959
01:25:05.360 --> 01:25:06.694
without my head feeling like it was

1960
01:25:06.694 --> 01:25:07.527
gonna explode.
You couldn't equalize

1961
01:25:07.527 --> 01:25:09.430
properly?
I could not equalize.

1962
01:25:09.430 --> 01:25:12.980
So I'm sure with discipline and training,

1963
01:25:12.980 --> 01:25:14.947
I could get past that.

1964
01:25:14.947 --> 01:25:15.950
But on that day,

1965
01:25:15.950 --> 01:25:17.740
which is the last time I tried it, I couldn't.

1966
01:25:17.740 --> 01:25:20.410
So I don't feel like I could really get

1967
01:25:20.410 --> 01:25:22.840
the full freediving experience

1968
01:25:22.840 --> 01:25:24.670
because of my inability to equalize.

1969
01:25:24.670 --> 01:25:26.807
Yeah, you just have to work on it a little bit.

1970
01:25:26.807 --> 01:25:28.810
You can easily get past it.

1971
01:25:28.810 --> 01:25:30.070
But I love the monofin.

1972
01:25:30.070 --> 01:25:32.780
All of which brings us to the show and tell,

1973
01:25:32.780 --> 01:25:34.160
which is that-
Yes, not a fin.

1974
01:25:34.160 --> 01:25:36.610
John, who then, I've just recounted

1975
01:25:36.610 --> 01:25:39.780
the early days of FINIS,

1976
01:25:39.780 --> 01:25:41.570
which was a company that literally

1977
01:25:41.570 --> 01:25:44.800
started with a monofin as its product

1978
01:25:44.800 --> 01:25:46.070
has now become this robust

1979
01:25:46.070 --> 01:25:49.920
competitive swimming apparel line,

1980
01:25:49.920 --> 01:25:52.760
has come out with a smart goggle.

1981
01:25:52.760 --> 01:25:55.700
This is what it looks like.
Look at that thing.

1982
01:25:55.700 --> 01:25:57.843
And John sent me one of the early,

1983
01:25:58.970 --> 01:26:00.090
before it's commercial,

1984
01:26:00.090 --> 01:26:01.587
it's not quite commercially available for you.

1985
01:26:01.587 --> 01:26:04.260
You can pre-order it, they're shipping in May.

1986
01:26:04.260 --> 01:26:08.300
But what's cool about it is it has a computer

1987
01:26:08.300 --> 01:26:09.900
that you can see that that fits in here.

1988
01:26:09.900 --> 01:26:14.290
You kind of push it in here and it clicks in.

1989
01:26:14.290 --> 01:26:15.963
And it's got a little optical,

1990
01:26:17.740 --> 01:26:19.840
I don't know what you would call that, lens there.

1991
01:26:19.840 --> 01:26:22.860
And in the corner for your left eye,

1992
01:26:22.860 --> 01:26:27.460
it provides output and data on your swim workout,

1993
01:26:27.460 --> 01:26:29.680
essentially real-time data to better understand

1994
01:26:29.680 --> 01:26:31.100
the patterns in your swim training.

1995
01:26:31.100 --> 01:26:34.560
It calculates split and time and distance.

1996
01:26:34.560 --> 01:26:37.820
You can see it very easily and read it pretty easily.

1997
01:26:37.820 --> 01:26:40.020
I was concerned because I wear glasses.

1998
01:26:40.020 --> 01:26:41.130
I'm pretty blind without them.

1999
01:26:41.130 --> 01:26:43.070
But I had no trouble reading it.

2000
01:26:43.070 --> 01:26:45.170
And it's amazing how it kind of knows,

2001
01:26:45.170 --> 01:26:47.380
if you're doing a set of hundreds,

2002
01:26:47.380 --> 01:26:49.250
it knows that if you sit on the wall

2003
01:26:49.250 --> 01:26:51.400
in between each hundred repeat

2004
01:26:51.400 --> 01:26:52.760
for more than like five seconds,

2005
01:26:52.760 --> 01:26:55.390
it's like, oh, okay, he's taking a break.

2006
01:26:55.390 --> 01:26:57.570
This is your split for the hundred.

2007
01:26:57.570 --> 01:27:00.470
And you don't really need a pace clock,

2008
01:27:00.470 --> 01:27:02.010
because it'll then count down

2009
01:27:02.890 --> 01:27:05.700
how much rest you want in between each one of those.

2010
01:27:05.700 --> 01:27:08.900
And then it all syncs up with this app

2011
01:27:08.900 --> 01:27:12.790
called the Ciye, C-I-Y-E app.

2012
01:27:12.790 --> 01:27:14.590
So your workouts upload there.

2013
01:27:14.590 --> 01:27:16.670
It integrates with Strava if you'd like that.

2014
01:27:16.670 --> 01:27:19.180
And what's cool and different about this smart goggle,

2015
01:27:19.180 --> 01:27:22.120
'cause there are other smart goggles out there

2016
01:27:22.120 --> 01:27:25.760
is that the actual technology, you can pull it out.

2017
01:27:25.760 --> 01:27:27.787
'Cause the goggle at some point it's gonna wear out.

2018
01:27:27.787 --> 01:27:29.670
The lens are gonna get scratched

2019
01:27:29.670 --> 01:27:32.690
and you're gonna break the mooring or whatever.

2020
01:27:32.690 --> 01:27:34.570
You just get another pair of goggles

2021
01:27:34.570 --> 01:27:36.510
and pop in the tech into that.

2022
01:27:36.510 --> 01:27:37.510
Okay.
Which is cool.

2023
01:27:37.510 --> 01:27:40.320
Perfect.
So I dig it.

2024
01:27:40.320 --> 01:27:41.153
I dig FINIS.

2025
01:27:41.153 --> 01:27:43.650
I feel allegiance to them because of my history

2026
01:27:43.650 --> 01:27:45.060
with John and the whole finswimming.

2027
01:27:45.060 --> 01:27:46.170
'Cause of your hungry days.

2028
01:27:46.170 --> 01:27:47.003
My hungry days.

2029
01:27:47.003 --> 01:27:48.660
It all comes in this beautiful box

2030
01:27:48.660 --> 01:27:49.980
and it's pretty easy to set up.

2031
01:27:49.980 --> 01:27:52.760
I set it up the other day and I dig it.

2032
01:27:52.760 --> 01:27:56.780
So another tool for the swimmers out there.

2033
01:27:56.780 --> 01:27:57.770
You know what I dig?

2034
01:27:57.770 --> 01:27:59.680
Your Rolodex.

2035
01:27:59.680 --> 01:28:00.943
You have a very impressive Rolodex, sir.

2036
01:28:00.943 --> 01:28:04.210
Listen, I'm 54, so I've lived a little bit of life

2037
01:28:04.210 --> 01:28:06.390
and I've met some people along the way.

2038
01:28:06.390 --> 01:28:08.250
Companies founders,

2039
01:28:08.250 --> 01:28:09.390
Minneapolis.
I love talking about-

2040
01:28:09.390 --> 01:28:10.410
Fixers.
finswimming, though.

2041
01:28:10.410 --> 01:28:12.450
I wish could remember, I'm gonna have to go back.

2042
01:28:12.450 --> 01:28:16.280
I have a photo album that I created from that experience

