2450
01:45:11.610 --> 01:45:12.490
They should have one.

2451
01:45:12.490 --> 01:45:14.280
It's such a cool city

2452
01:45:14.280 --> 01:45:15.980
and there's so much water surrounding it

2453
01:45:15.980 --> 01:45:19.120
that you could like run down these historic streets-

2454
01:45:19.120 --> 01:45:20.670
That'd be amazing.
And then jump in the water,

2455
01:45:20.670 --> 01:45:22.210
and I don't know, I think it would be cool.

2456
01:45:22.210 --> 01:45:23.490
It'd be amazing.

2457
01:45:23.490 --> 01:45:24.930
Steven, thank you for your question.

2458
01:45:24.930 --> 01:45:29.240
Listen, post-race blues is a normal response.

2459
01:45:29.240 --> 01:45:30.890
Any athlete who's been in the game

2460
01:45:30.890 --> 01:45:33.910
for any period of time has peaks and valleys,

2461
01:45:33.910 --> 01:45:36.130
you hit plateaus, et cetera.

2462
01:45:36.130 --> 01:45:39.390
So I think you're, first of all,

2463
01:45:39.390 --> 01:45:41.610
it's okay that you feel that way.

2464
01:45:41.610 --> 01:45:43.720
Every athlete has gone through that.

2465
01:45:43.720 --> 01:45:45.030
It's not abnormal.

2466
01:45:45.030 --> 01:45:49.030
So I wouldn't beat yourself up for being in that spot.

2467
01:45:49.030 --> 01:45:52.650
And not only is it okay for you to take a break,

2468
01:45:52.650 --> 01:45:54.680
I would advise taking a break.

2469
01:45:54.680 --> 01:45:57.420
You can't just power through race

2470
01:45:57.420 --> 01:45:59.130
after race after race after race

2471
01:45:59.130 --> 01:46:01.520
and it's just this endless upward trajectory.

2472
01:46:01.520 --> 01:46:03.150
It doesn't work that way.

2473
01:46:03.150 --> 01:46:08.150
You have to build in periods of rest, periods of recovery.

2474
01:46:09.080 --> 01:46:14.080
And that kind of mentality should apply to how you plan out

2475
01:46:16.160 --> 01:46:20.200
not just your training blocks week by week,

2476
01:46:20.200 --> 01:46:22.740
but also day by day and year by year.

2477
01:46:22.740 --> 01:46:24.550
These things are seasonal, right?

2478
01:46:24.550 --> 01:46:29.550
And you can't, if longevity is an aim or a goal,

2479
01:46:29.900 --> 01:46:32.030
you can't just hammer incessantly.

2480
01:46:32.030 --> 01:46:35.720
You've got to build this into your program.

2481
01:46:35.720 --> 01:46:37.760
And now that you've had a taste of this,

2482
01:46:37.760 --> 01:46:40.980
hopefully, you can understand why this is so important.

2483
01:46:40.980 --> 01:46:44.030
At the same time, you don't wanna be that guy

2484
01:46:44.030 --> 01:46:46.010
who's rubberbanding in and out of shape,

2485
01:46:46.010 --> 01:46:47.240
gaining weight, losing weight.

2486
01:46:47.240 --> 01:46:49.260
To some extent, that's okay,

2487
01:46:49.260 --> 01:46:52.010
but you don't wanna get too far out of it

2488
01:46:52.010 --> 01:46:54.150
that when you get back into it,

2489
01:46:54.150 --> 01:46:58.240
you have this very long period of trying to lose

2490
01:46:58.240 --> 01:47:00.110
a bunch of extra weight and get into shape.

2491
01:47:00.110 --> 01:47:03.560
You wanna stay in contact with your fitness,

2492
01:47:03.560 --> 01:47:05.810
so you never wanna move too far away from it,

2493
01:47:06.770 --> 01:47:08.470
because it's so much easier to stay in shape

2494
01:47:08.470 --> 01:47:10.299
than it is to get totally out of shape

2495
01:47:10.299 --> 01:47:12.550
and then spend all your time trying to get back in shape.

2496
01:47:12.550 --> 01:47:13.640
And that way, you never feel

2497
01:47:13.640 --> 01:47:15.970
like you're actually making progress.

2498
01:47:15.970 --> 01:47:19.790
So a couple suggestions that maybe might be helpful for you.

2499
01:47:19.790 --> 01:47:22.430
First of all, mix up your training.

2500
01:47:22.430 --> 01:47:25.830
And you can do this by cycling it seasonally,

2501
01:47:25.830 --> 01:47:27.680
like I mentioned.

2502
01:47:27.680 --> 01:47:29.060
Try different things.

2503
01:47:29.060 --> 01:47:30.150
If you're running all the time,

2504
01:47:30.150 --> 01:47:32.620
get on a bike or get in the water,

2505
01:47:32.620 --> 01:47:34.440
go to a yoga class or hit the weight,

2506
01:47:34.440 --> 01:47:37.450
or do something else that breaks up

2507
01:47:37.450 --> 01:47:40.020
the monotony of just running all the time,

2508
01:47:40.020 --> 01:47:41.520
I think is important.

2509
01:47:41.520 --> 01:47:45.300
And you got to keep it fun and not so serious.

2510
01:47:45.300 --> 01:47:48.250
If you're always in this performance mindset

2511
01:47:48.250 --> 01:47:52.000
of chasing these races and these goals,

2512
01:47:52.000 --> 01:47:54.577
it's easy to kind of lapse into this state

2513
01:47:57.820 --> 01:47:59.440
where you're taking yourself too seriously.

2514
01:47:59.440 --> 01:48:02.240
And every minute, you're focused on your training.

2515
01:48:02.240 --> 01:48:03.330
And you're gonna burn out.

2516
01:48:03.330 --> 01:48:05.970
So you've got to find the joy in all of it.

2517
01:48:05.970 --> 01:48:08.750
And I think if you can connect with that,

2518
01:48:08.750 --> 01:48:10.560
that will build some longevity

2519
01:48:10.560 --> 01:48:13.660
into this journey that you're on.

2520
01:48:13.660 --> 01:48:15.810
So again, mixing in some strengths,

2521
01:48:15.810 --> 01:48:18.630
some cross-training, other aerobic activities.

2522
01:48:18.630 --> 01:48:20.290
Summer's coming up in Charleston.

2523
01:48:20.290 --> 01:48:23.040
I'm sure there's things you can do on a boat.

2524
01:48:23.040 --> 01:48:25.140
I don't know what goes on there.

2525
01:48:25.140 --> 01:48:27.960
Steven, there is a swimrun challenge in June.

2526
01:48:27.960 --> 01:48:28.793
There you go.

2527
01:48:28.793 --> 01:48:30.350
I don't know What his deal is with swimming,

2528
01:48:30.350 --> 01:48:32.280
but maybe he can get into that.

2529
01:48:32.280 --> 01:48:35.180
I also think, and this is direct

2530
01:48:35.180 --> 01:48:40.080
from the creator of the 4x4x48, David Goggins himself.

2531
01:48:40.080 --> 01:48:43.730
It's important to stop over-indexing on motivation,

2532
01:48:43.730 --> 01:48:48.270
because motivation is an unreliable motivator.

2533
01:48:48.270 --> 01:48:49.890
It really is.

2534
01:48:49.890 --> 01:48:53.510
We tend to think, well, I'm not motivated, so I won't do it.

2535
01:48:53.510 --> 01:48:56.420
It's very fickle, it's very temporal,

2536
01:48:56.420 --> 01:49:01.420
and it shouldn't provide the foundation of your why.

2537
01:49:02.810 --> 01:49:06.650
So instead, I always tell people to think

2538
01:49:06.650 --> 01:49:10.690
more about it in the context of the values

2539
01:49:10.690 --> 01:49:12.130
that you're trying to prioritize.

2540
01:49:12.130 --> 01:49:15.660
So instead of saying I don't feel motivated,

2541
01:49:15.660 --> 01:49:18.900
the idea would be to say I am an athlete,

2542
01:49:18.900 --> 01:49:22.263
or I am somebody who does hard things.

2543
01:49:23.320 --> 01:49:25.150
To create identity around it,

2544
01:49:25.150 --> 01:49:29.970
as opposed to this fluctuating emotional state

2545
01:49:29.970 --> 01:49:32.060
that you can't really create for yourself.

2546
01:49:32.060 --> 01:49:34.730
And I think if you can contextualize

2547
01:49:34.730 --> 01:49:36.870
what you're doing in that manner,

2548
01:49:36.870 --> 01:49:40.800
you'll create a more sustainable fuel for yourself.

2549
01:49:40.800 --> 01:49:43.330
I also think that you need to ask yourself

2550
01:49:43.330 --> 01:49:46.700
if you're training simply to get a result,

2551
01:49:46.700 --> 01:49:49.330
to say you did it, or you cross that finish line,

2552
01:49:49.330 --> 01:49:50.960
or you hit that PR,

2553
01:49:50.960 --> 01:49:55.560
or are you training because it does bring you joy?

2554
01:49:55.560 --> 01:49:57.523
It's an expression of who you are.

2555
01:49:58.660 --> 01:50:03.120
Because if you think of yourself as an athlete

2556
01:50:03.120 --> 01:50:05.560
or somebody who does hard things,

2557
01:50:05.560 --> 01:50:07.860
then you understand that we don't train

2558
01:50:07.860 --> 01:50:09.530
when we're motivated to do so,

2559
01:50:09.530 --> 01:50:13.983
we train because this is who we are and what we do.

2560
01:50:13.983 --> 01:50:15.590
I like it.

2561
01:50:15.590 --> 01:50:16.423
That's all really good.

2562
01:50:16.423 --> 01:50:18.560
I would just add, or just not even add,

2563
01:50:18.560 --> 01:50:20.500
but just emphasize what you said.

2564
01:50:20.500 --> 01:50:22.113
I think David would tell you,

2565
01:50:23.580 --> 01:50:25.850
if your brain is saying I don't wanna do it,

2566
01:50:25.850 --> 01:50:27.420
then you flip that right away and say

2567
01:50:27.420 --> 01:50:29.780
if that's what he hears, he's right out the door.

2568
01:50:29.780 --> 01:50:31.082
If he hears, I don't wanna

2569
01:50:31.082 --> 01:50:32.320
fucking do this shit,
That's the muscle.

2570
01:50:32.320 --> 01:50:35.690
He's like, that means you have to go do it,

2571
01:50:35.690 --> 01:50:37.620
and you just force yourself to do it.

2572
01:50:37.620 --> 01:50:39.700
And it does take time, like you say.

2573
01:50:39.700 --> 01:50:41.180
It's the same thing you're saying.

2574
01:50:41.180 --> 01:50:42.270
Telling yourself you're an athlete

2575
01:50:42.270 --> 01:50:43.590
or I'm someone who does hard things

2576
01:50:43.590 --> 01:50:44.500
is the same thing as saying,

2577
01:50:44.500 --> 01:50:45.940
well, I don't wanna do it today.

2578
01:50:45.940 --> 01:50:47.990
Oh, that means I have to do it today.

2579
01:50:47.990 --> 01:50:50.250
So it's just however you want to do it,

2580
01:50:50.250 --> 01:50:52.500
you're training the brain to flip it.

2581
01:50:52.500 --> 01:50:53.950
And I think he'd say that.

2582
01:50:53.950 --> 01:50:55.600
And the other thing I would say

2583
01:50:55.600 --> 01:50:58.660
is I'm big believer in cross-training,

2584
01:50:58.660 --> 01:51:00.520
just like you said, I think it really does help.

2585
01:51:00.520 --> 01:51:02.220
It brings the fun into it.

2586
01:51:02.220 --> 01:51:05.830
So whether it's swimming or riding a bike or whatever it is.

2587
01:51:05.830 --> 01:51:10.830
And then the zone two training.

2588
01:51:11.690 --> 01:51:13.223
Maybe he's running too hard.

2589
01:51:14.120 --> 01:51:17.450
Zone two, I found I enjoy running

2590
01:51:17.450 --> 01:51:19.270
in a way that I never enjoyed it before.

2591
01:51:19.270 --> 01:51:20.800
Kinda takes the pressure off.

2592
01:51:20.800 --> 01:51:23.700
You can feel tired and it's late in the day,

2593
01:51:23.700 --> 01:51:26.887
but you're like, well, it's just a zone two run.

2594
01:51:26.887 --> 01:51:27.720
You don't have to sweat it.

2595
01:51:27.720 --> 01:51:30.190
Don't be fixated on your garment

2596
01:51:30.190 --> 01:51:32.030
and all the kind of devices

2597
01:51:32.030 --> 01:51:34.520
and hold yourself to some standard

2598
01:51:34.520 --> 01:51:36.520
that every time you go out to train,

2599
01:51:36.520 --> 01:51:37.860
you've got to be better and faster

2600
01:51:37.860 --> 01:51:39.210
than you were the day before.

2601
01:51:39.210 --> 01:51:40.820
Right, if you just decide,

2602
01:51:40.820 --> 01:51:44.915
I'm gonna lay back and do an hour of zone two,

2603
01:51:44.915 --> 01:51:47.470
then you still do get a sweat.

2604
01:51:47.470 --> 01:51:48.977
You still do burn calories.

2605
01:51:48.977 --> 01:51:51.230
You still do get some positive benefits.

2606
01:51:51.230 --> 01:51:53.240
When you're going on heart rate, too,

2607
01:51:53.240 --> 01:51:55.990
you're not measuring yourself against a pace

2608
01:51:55.990 --> 01:51:58.580
where then you either feel bad or good about yourself,

2609
01:51:58.580 --> 01:51:59.590
dictated by that.

2610
01:51:59.590 --> 01:52:01.090
You're just training where you're at.

2611
01:52:01.090 --> 01:52:02.770
You're not training where you think you should be

2612
01:52:02.770 --> 01:52:04.930
or where you were last year at this time.

2613
01:52:04.930 --> 01:52:08.000
Train where you're at.
Love it, good one.

2614
01:52:08.000 --> 01:52:09.410
Great question.

2615
01:52:09.410 --> 01:52:10.450
I should have prefaced this.

2616
01:52:10.450 --> 01:52:13.430
All these questions are kind of run-oriented,

2617
01:52:13.430 --> 01:52:14.400
which is good.

2618
01:52:14.400 --> 01:52:17.150
I'm starting to try to curate this moment.

2619
01:52:17.150 --> 01:52:19.110
No finswimming questions today?

2620
01:52:19.110 --> 01:52:19.943
No, we've had enough mermen-

2621
01:52:19.943 --> 01:52:21.683
I'm training world championships in finswimming, Rich.

2622
01:52:22.930 --> 01:52:24.690
Enough merman talk for one day.

2623
01:52:24.690 --> 01:52:28.200
You guys were like the OG Instagram roommates.

2624
01:52:28.200 --> 01:52:29.070
Yeah, I don't know.

2625
01:52:29.070 --> 01:52:31.980
There was barely an internet back then.

2626
01:52:31.980 --> 01:52:33.680
You were the OG internet mermen.

2627
01:52:34.683 --> 01:52:37.090
All right, Casey from Illinois.

2628
01:52:37.090 --> 01:52:40.190
Hello, Rich and Adam, this is Casey from Illinois.

2629
01:52:40.190 --> 01:52:42.160
And I am running the Chicago Marathon

2630
01:52:42.160 --> 01:52:44.130
for the first time in October.

2631
01:52:44.130 --> 01:52:45.350
And I'm just wondering if you've had

2632
01:52:45.350 --> 01:52:50.260
any after-race, after-run tips.

2633
01:52:50.260 --> 01:52:52.240
And thank you for all you do.

2634
01:52:52.240 --> 01:52:54.280
I love the podcast and the books, thank you.

2635
01:52:54.280 --> 01:52:55.140
Thanks, Casey.

2636
01:52:55.140 --> 01:52:58.990
Did he say after-race, after-run?

2637
01:52:58.990 --> 01:53:01.870
I thought it was just any race than an after-run tips,

2638
01:53:01.870 --> 01:53:04.080
but let's just go with what you got.

2639
01:53:05.100 --> 01:53:06.740
Right, 'cause I saw this typed out

2640
01:53:06.740 --> 01:53:08.060
before I listened to it,

2641
01:53:08.060 --> 01:53:10.210
and now I'm wondering whether I need to rethink this.

2642
01:53:10.210 --> 01:53:11.580
But I'm gonna answer it the way

2643
01:53:11.580 --> 01:53:13.039
that I feel like answering it.
There you go.

2644
01:53:13.039 --> 01:53:14.700
Hopefully, this is helpful to Casey.

2645
01:53:14.700 --> 01:53:17.260
First of all, with respect to race tips,

2646
01:53:17.260 --> 01:53:19.340
there's this maxim that you shouldn't do anything

2647
01:53:19.340 --> 01:53:21.500
on race day that you didn't do in training.

2648
01:53:21.500 --> 01:53:24.520
Race day is not the time to test out

2649
01:53:24.520 --> 01:53:27.510
a fancy new pair of shoes or a new pacing strategy

2650
01:53:27.510 --> 01:53:30.700
or some kind of new nutrition protocol

2651
01:53:30.700 --> 01:53:33.900
or some gel that you've never tried before.

2652
01:53:33.900 --> 01:53:36.430
Training is all about testing, testing, testing

2653
01:53:36.430 --> 01:53:38.900
so that by the time you reach your race,

2654
01:53:38.900 --> 01:53:40.680
you already know exactly what you're doing.

2655
01:53:40.680 --> 01:53:44.290
The race is merely the execution

2656
01:53:44.290 --> 01:53:45.530
of what you've already done.

2657
01:53:45.530 --> 01:53:49.520
So if you've trained properly and you visualized it,

2658
01:53:49.520 --> 01:53:53.130
then it just becomes about showing up, enjoying yourself,

2659
01:53:53.130 --> 01:53:55.920
and celebrating the journey that got you there.

2660
01:53:55.920 --> 01:53:59.240
So having fun, enjoying it I think is super important.

2661
01:53:59.240 --> 01:54:01.840
The whole idea of running a marathon

2662
01:54:01.840 --> 01:54:04.070
is to have a positive experience.

2663
01:54:04.070 --> 01:54:05.890
It's gonna hurt of course,

2664
01:54:05.890 --> 01:54:07.380
but if and when you feel like it's all

2665
01:54:07.380 --> 01:54:08.940
falling apart and you feel terrible,

2666
01:54:08.940 --> 01:54:11.500
you should also know that that isn't necessarily

2667
01:54:11.500 --> 01:54:14.620
a predictor of anything because in a long race,

2668
01:54:14.620 --> 01:54:16.180
things change, you can feel terrible,

2669
01:54:16.180 --> 01:54:18.920
then you'll feel good, then you'll feel terrible again.

2670
01:54:18.920 --> 01:54:22.200
And you learn to just keep going no matter what.

2671
01:54:22.200 --> 01:54:26.360
And with that, to adapt to any unforeseen variables

2672
01:54:26.360 --> 01:54:28.090
that get thrown in your direction.

2673
01:54:28.090 --> 01:54:30.970
A lot of times, athletes, they visualize,

2674
01:54:30.970 --> 01:54:33.670
they train and they're very attached

2675
01:54:33.670 --> 01:54:36.247
to the race unfolding in a very specific way.

2676
01:54:36.247 --> 01:54:38.500
And the minute something goes wrong,

2677
01:54:38.500 --> 01:54:41.080
like you step in a puddle and suddenly your foot's all wet,

2678
01:54:41.080 --> 01:54:44.730
or I don't know, somebody bumps you when you fall down,

2679
01:54:44.730 --> 01:54:48.800
anything unforeseen can throw somebody off their mental game

2680
01:54:48.800 --> 01:54:51.980
and they have a hard time kind of getting back into it.

2681
01:54:51.980 --> 01:54:55.010
So I think sort of understanding

2682
01:54:55.010 --> 01:54:57.470
that there's gonna be variables that get thrown in your way

2683
01:54:57.470 --> 01:54:59.480
and learning how to be resilient with those

2684
01:54:59.480 --> 01:55:02.950
is super important and something to prepare

2685
01:55:02.950 --> 01:55:05.293
mentally and physically for in your training.

2686
01:55:06.450 --> 01:55:09.700
In addition, on race day, you should know

2687
01:55:09.700 --> 01:55:12.520
what your strategy is and you should implement that.

2688
01:55:12.520 --> 01:55:14.320
Meaning, run your race,

2689
01:55:14.320 --> 01:55:17.670
not the race that the person next to you is running.

2690
01:55:17.670 --> 01:55:20.050
It's very easy, especially when there's a lot of people

2691
01:55:20.050 --> 01:55:22.680
around you and people are passing you

2692
01:55:22.680 --> 01:55:25.530
that you can get caught up in what others are doing.

2693
01:55:25.530 --> 01:55:30.530
And it takes a certain level of confidence

2694
01:55:30.530 --> 01:55:33.890
and discipline to not let any of that

2695
01:55:33.890 --> 01:55:37.210
impact your plan that you've set for yourself.

2696
01:55:37.210 --> 01:55:39.320
So sticking to your plan is key.

2697
01:55:39.320 --> 01:55:42.310
And that also means that on race day,

2698
01:55:42.310 --> 01:55:43.810
you're gonna have all this extra energy.

2699
01:55:43.810 --> 01:55:44.930
You're gonna be very excited.

2700
01:55:44.930 --> 01:55:47.960
There's gonna be a lot of crowd and whatever.

2701
01:55:47.960 --> 01:55:49.800
And if you find yourself feeling like,

2702
01:55:49.800 --> 01:55:52.780
you're five miles in and you feel amazing,

2703
01:55:52.780 --> 01:55:55.070
a lot of athletes throw their race plan out the window

2704
01:55:55.070 --> 01:55:57.180
and just start speeding up 'cause they feel great

2705
01:55:57.180 --> 01:55:59.770
only to like explode at mile 18.

2706
01:55:59.770 --> 01:56:02.640
So if you feel amazing, resist that urge

2707
01:56:02.640 --> 01:56:06.210
to go faster and stick to your plan.

2708
01:56:06.210 --> 01:56:09.700
In terms of after race, which I think is really

2709
01:56:09.700 --> 01:56:14.700
the core of what Casey wants answers to.

2710
01:56:15.280 --> 01:56:17.570
I think after your race, it's important to do

2711
01:56:17.570 --> 01:56:20.340
an inventory of what went right and what went wrong.

2712
01:56:20.340 --> 01:56:24.950
You can write it out in a journal, just in freehand,

2713
01:56:24.950 --> 01:56:27.880
everything that that you thought about,

2714
01:56:27.880 --> 01:56:31.670
every emotional experience, what it felt like physically,

2715
01:56:31.670 --> 01:56:34.490
where it went to plan, where it didn't go to plan.

2716
01:56:34.490 --> 01:56:35.940
You can mine the data.

2717
01:56:35.940 --> 01:56:40.540
And all of that is an ample opportunity

2718
01:56:40.540 --> 01:56:44.010
to learn from the experience that you just had.

2719
01:56:44.010 --> 01:56:48.710
But the important thing is in the aftermath of the race,

2720
01:56:48.710 --> 01:56:52.290
have fun, go to the Beer Garden, whatever, enjoy yourself,

2721
01:56:52.290 --> 01:56:57.290
make sure you hydrate, fuel up, stay warm, ice your legs.

2722
01:56:57.290 --> 01:56:58.910
I think active recovery in the days

2723
01:56:58.910 --> 01:57:00.560
that follow is super important.

2724
01:57:00.560 --> 01:57:02.270
If it's your first marathon,

2725
01:57:02.270 --> 01:57:04.130
you're gonna be unbelievably stiff

2726
01:57:04.130 --> 01:57:06.250
and have trouble going up and down stairs

2727
01:57:06.250 --> 01:57:07.350
or even walking around.

2728
01:57:07.350 --> 01:57:09.560
But you've got to, you shouldn't just lay in bed.

2729
01:57:09.560 --> 01:57:12.000
You should get up and move and go on walks

2730
01:57:12.000 --> 01:57:15.860
and try to get the blood flowing in a low-grade way.

2731
01:57:15.860 --> 01:57:18.190
And that'll help flush out all the lactic acid

2732
01:57:18.190 --> 01:57:20.900
and help you recover a little bit more quickly.

2733
01:57:20.900 --> 01:57:22.950
But there's no need to go running for,

2734
01:57:22.950 --> 01:57:25.900
I don't know, a week or 10 days or something like that.

2735
01:57:25.900 --> 01:57:27.020
Allow your body to heal.

2736
01:57:27.020 --> 01:57:29.500
And I think another mistake a lot of athletes make

2737
01:57:29.500 --> 01:57:33.130
is they wanna get right back into it too quickly.

2738
01:57:33.130 --> 01:57:37.530
And they underestimate just how much their body

2739
01:57:37.530 --> 01:57:41.230
gets beat up in running something like a marathon.

2740
01:57:41.230 --> 01:57:44.330
So giving your body the space and the time

2741
01:57:44.330 --> 01:57:48.550
to repair itself takes longer than you might imagine.

2742
01:57:48.550 --> 01:57:50.130
And providing that opportunity

2743
01:57:50.130 --> 01:57:51.830
for yourself I think is important.

2744
01:57:53.120 --> 01:57:54.270
Go to the Beer Garden.

2745
01:57:54.270 --> 01:57:57.470
Advice I never thought I'd hear Rich Roll deliver on.

2746
01:57:57.470 --> 01:57:58.498
I'm not going to the Beer,

2747
01:57:58.498 --> 01:57:59.770
well, I can go to the Beer Garden,

2748
01:57:59.770 --> 01:58:00.900
I'm not gonna drink the beer,

2749
01:58:00.900 --> 01:58:04.500
but he can drink the beer if that's what he wants to do.

2750
01:58:04.500 --> 01:58:08.063
I always enter any sort of race with one plan.

2751
01:58:09.190 --> 01:58:11.490
Just don't come in last.

2752
01:58:11.490 --> 01:58:13.410
Why, well, what's wrong with that?

2753
01:58:13.410 --> 01:58:14.630
What's wrong with coming in last?

2754
01:58:14.630 --> 01:58:15.950
The lanterne rouge?

2755
01:58:15.950 --> 01:58:20.560
I'm sure as I age, I will even let that go. (laughs)

2756
01:58:20.560 --> 01:58:23.840
This is the extent of your competitive fire.

2757
01:58:23.840 --> 01:58:26.738
Don't come in last.
The extent of my egotism.

2758
01:58:26.738 --> 01:58:30.550
I would suggest not even thinking about that at all,

2759
01:58:30.550 --> 01:58:33.300
because that means that you're gauging

2760
01:58:33.300 --> 01:58:34.603
your performance off of other people.

2761
01:58:34.603 --> 01:58:35.760
I know, it's true.

2762
01:58:35.760 --> 01:58:37.580
You should just think about executing

2763
01:58:37.580 --> 01:58:40.880
your race the way that you trained for.

2764
01:58:40.880 --> 01:58:44.140
And I'm glad we didn't get into my shaming

2765
01:58:44.140 --> 01:58:46.710
of not wearing goggles when I swim.

2766
01:58:46.710 --> 01:58:48.140
We'll have to do that another time.

2767
01:58:48.140 --> 01:58:50.260
Yeah, oh, that's right, we didn't even talk about that.

2768
01:58:50.260 --> 01:58:52.690
So Adam and I, we'll quickly recap this

2769
01:58:52.690 --> 01:58:54.713
before the final question.

2770
01:58:55.740 --> 01:58:57.360
I'm talking to Adam on the phone the other night

2771
01:58:57.360 --> 01:59:01.290
and he's telling me about how his face mask is big.

2772
01:59:01.290 --> 01:59:02.123
What do you call?

2773
01:59:02.123 --> 01:59:03.420
I don't even know you-

2774
01:59:03.420 --> 01:59:05.120
I took three waves on the head

2775
01:59:05.120 --> 01:59:08.570
on my way in from my last swim, which was fun.

2776
01:59:08.570 --> 01:59:10.810
I enjoyed getting thrashed and ragdolled,

2777
01:59:10.810 --> 01:59:12.540
because it was not that big a surf,

2778
01:59:12.540 --> 01:59:16.070
but I did get messed up and I lost my mask.

2779
01:59:16.070 --> 01:59:18.500
I wear a mask.
He wears a mask.

2780
01:59:18.500 --> 01:59:22.560
So I just can't, I'm like, listen, buddy, all right,

2781
01:59:22.560 --> 01:59:25.970
as somebody who's competed in swimrun competitions,

2782
01:59:25.970 --> 01:59:29.680
from an etiquette perspective,

2783
01:59:29.680 --> 01:59:32.320
a real swimmer would never wear a face mask.

2784
01:59:32.320 --> 01:59:33.153
I'm a breaker of etiquette.

2785
01:59:33.153 --> 01:59:36.700
So get on the goggles and dispatch this.

2786
01:59:36.700 --> 01:59:38.250
It's good that it got lost.

2787
01:59:38.250 --> 01:59:39.970
It's time, all right.

2788
01:59:39.970 --> 01:59:42.230
It's embarrassing to go swimming with you in the ocean

2789
01:59:42.230 --> 01:59:43.990
and you're wearing a face mask.

2790
01:59:43.990 --> 01:59:46.200
You are not the Octopus Teacher.

2791
01:59:46.200 --> 01:59:48.410
Wait.
You are a swimrun athlete.

2792
01:59:48.410 --> 01:59:51.270
No, no, no, no, let me push back just for a second.

2793
01:59:51.270 --> 01:59:52.760
I wear a Sphera mask

2794
01:59:52.760 --> 01:59:54.730
by Aqua Sphere.
I don't care.

2795
01:59:54.730 --> 01:59:55.563
What are you talking about?

2796
01:59:55.563 --> 01:59:56.790
Well, the Sphera mask

2797
01:59:56.790 --> 01:59:59.590
was designed for open water swimmers.

2798
01:59:59.590 --> 02:00:03.350
Listen, Aqua Sphere also makes goggles-

2799
02:00:03.350 --> 02:00:05.290
They do.
For open water swimming.

2800
02:00:05.290 --> 02:00:09.090
But I would highly suggest the FINIS line of goggles-

2801
02:00:09.090 --> 02:00:11.440
FINIS.
Including the smart goggle.

